'''Thomas Hawley Tuberville''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|t|ʌ|b|ər|v|ɪ|l}};<ref>{{Cite web |title='We need a different voice:' Tommy Tuberville says it's time to send real people to Washington D.C. |url=https://www.cbs42.com/news/politics/your-local-election-hq/we-need-a-different-voice-tommy-tuberville-says-its-time-to-send-real-people-to-washington-d-c/ |url-status=live |date=March 4, 2020 |website=CBS 42 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200304141329/https://www.cbs42.com/news/politics/your-local-election-hq/we-need-a-different-voice-tommy-tuberville-says-its-time-to-send-real-people-to-washington-d-c/ |first=Jessalyn |last=Adams |language=en-US |access-date=May 16, 2020 |archive-date=March 4, 2020 }}</ref> {{respell|TUB|ər|vil}}; born September 18, 1954) is an American politician and retired [[college football]] coach and sports broadcaster who is the [[Seniority in the United States Senate|senior]] [[United States senator]] from [[Alabama]], a seat he has held since 2021. He is a member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]]. Before entering politics, Tuberville was the head football coach at [[Auburn University]] from 1999 to 2008. He was also the head football coach at the [[University of Mississippi]] from 1995 to 1998, [[Texas Tech University]] from 2010 to 2012, and the [[University of Cincinnati]] from 2013 to 2016.

Tuberville won five national coach-of-the-year awards ([[Associated Press College Football Coach of the Year Award|AP]], [[AFCA Coach of the Year Award|AFCA]], [[Sporting News College Football Coach of the Year|Sporting News]], [[Walter Camp Coach of the Year|Walter Camp]], and [[Paul "Bear" Bryant Award|Bear Bryant]]) after [[2004 Auburn Tigers football team|Auburn's 13–0 season]] in 2004, in which Auburn won the [[Southeastern Conference]] title and the [[2005 Sugar Bowl|Sugar Bowl]], but was left out of the [[BCS National Championship Game]].{{Efn|In 2025, Auburn retroactively claimed the national championship for the 2004 season.}} He earned his 100th career win in 2007. Tuberville is the only [[Auburn Tigers football|Auburn football]] coach to beat [[Iron Bowl|in-state rival]] [[Alabama Crimson Tide football|Alabama]] six consecutive times. In 2015, he was the president of the [[American Football Coaches Association]]. He worked for [[ESPN]] as a [[color analyst]] for its [[ESPN College Football|college football coverage]] during 2017.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/20103323/espn-adds-tommy-tuberville-college-football-analyst|title=ESPN adds Tommy Tuberville as college football analyst. He currently hosts a radio talk show for wearebackroads sports network|work=ESPN.com|date=July 19, 2017|access-date=September 2, 2017|archive-date=November 19, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221119191404/https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/20103323/espn-adds-tommy-tuberville-college-football-analyst|url-status=live}}</ref>

In his first political campaign, Tuberville ran for Senate in [[2020 United States Senate election in Alabama|2020]], winning the Republican primary and defeating [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] incumbent [[Doug Jones (politician)|Doug Jones]]. Establishing himself as an ally of President [[Donald Trump]], he was among a group of Republican senators who voted to [[Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election|object to the certification]] of the [[2020 United States presidential election|2020 presidential election]]. In 2023, in protest of a [[United States Department of Defense|Defense Department]] policy reimbursing travel for soldiers seeking abortions, Tuberville blocked all promotions of senior officers in the U.S. military for 10 months, delaying over 450 promotions.<ref name="aug23">{{cite web |last=Skove |first=Sam |date=August 4, 2023 |title=Army now 2nd service without Senate-confirmed leader |url=https://www.defenseone.com/policy/2023/08/army-now-2nd-service-without-senate-confirmed-leader/389158/ |access-date=August 14, 2023 |work=Defense One}}</ref><ref name="armyNavyAug23">{{cite web |last1=Katz |first1=Justin |last2=Rocque |first2=Ashley |date=August 3, 2023 |title=3 acting chiefs, 3 black boxes: Army, Navy to join Marines without confirmed chief |url=https://breakingdefense.com/2023/08/3-acting-chiefs-3-black-boxes-army-navy-to-join-marines-without-confirmed-chief/ |access-date=August 14, 2023 |work=Breaking Defense |archive-date=August 21, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230821145501/https://breakingdefense.com/2023/08/3-acting-chiefs-3-black-boxes-army-navy-to-join-marines-without-confirmed-chief/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="Nazzaro 2023 p915">{{cite web |last=Nazzaro |first=Miranda |date=2023-08-14 |title=Three military services now without Senate-confirmed heads for first time in history |url=https://thehill.com/policy/defense/4151935-three-military-services-now-without-senate-confirmed-heads-for-first-time-in-history/ |access-date=2023-08-16 |website=The Hill |archive-date=December 8, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241208043215/https://thehill.com/policy/defense/4151935-three-military-services-now-without-senate-confirmed-heads-for-first-time-in-history/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He initially planned to [[2026 United States Senate election in Alabama|run for reelection]] to a second term, but later announced he would run for [[governor of Alabama]] in [[2026 Alabama gubernatorial election|2026]] instead.<ref>{{cite news |last=Beggin |first=Riley |date=May 27, 2025 |title=Tommy Tuberville, ex-Auburn football coach, announces 2026 campaign for Alabama governor |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2025/05/27/tommy-tuberville-gop-governor-alabama-2026/83870603007/ |work=USA Today |archive-date=June 1, 2025 |access-date=May 27, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250601232311/https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2025/05/27/tommy-tuberville-gop-governor-alabama-2026/83870603007/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

== Early life and education ==
Tuberville was born and raised in [[Camden, Arkansas]], one of three children of Olive Nell (née Chambliss) and Charles R. Tuberville Jr.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.legacy.com/us/obituaries/tullahomanews/name/olive-tuberville-obituary?id=17633561 |access-date=July 24, 2023 |title=Olive Tuberville Obituary (1928 - 2016) |work=Tullahoma News |via=legacy.com }}</ref> He graduated from [[Harmony Grove High School (Camden, Arkansas)|Harmony Grove High School]] in Camden in 1972.<ref name=":3">{{Cite news|last=Brassil|first=Gillian R.|date=July 15, 2020|title=Tuberville Advances: Will Alabama Send an Auburn Coach to the Senate?|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/07/15/sports/tommy-tuberville-alabama-auburn.html|access-date=December 9, 2020|issn=0362-4331|archive-date=February 10, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210210021722/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/07/15/sports/tommy-tuberville-alabama-auburn.html|url-status=live}}</ref> He attended Southern State College (now [[Southern Arkansas University]]), where he lettered in football as a safety for the Muleriders<ref name=":3" /> and played two years on the golf team. He received a B.S. in [[physical education]] from SSC in 1976.<ref name="grfx">{{Cite web |title=2014 Cincinnati Football |url=http://grfx.cstv.com/photos/schools/cinn/sports/m-footbl/auto_pdf/2014-15/misc_non_event/14_fb_guide.pdf |date=September 28, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140928125645/http://grfx.cstv.com/photos/schools/cinn/sports/m-footbl/auto_pdf/2014-15/misc_non_event/14_fb_guide.pdf |archive-date=September 28, 2014}}</ref> In 2008, he was inducted into the Southern Arkansas University Sports Hall of Fame and the Arkansas Sports Hall of Fame.<ref>{{cite press release |title=Tommy Tuberville Inducted Into Southern Arkansas University Sports Hall Of Fame |url=http://www.auburntigers.com/sports/m-footbl/spec-rel/102708aaa.html |date=October 27, 2008 |website=Auburn Football |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141224130412/http://www.auburntigers.com/sports/m-footbl/spec-rel/102708aaa.html |archive-date=December 24, 2014}}</ref>

==Coaching career==
[[File:President George W. Bush speaks to reporters with NCAA head football coaches.jpg|thumb|left| From left to right, [[Jack Siedlecki]] of Yale University, [[Mark Richt]] of the University of Georgia, [[Randy Shannon]] of the University of Miami, Tommy Tuberville of Auburn University and [[Charlie Weis]] of the University of Notre Dame watch President [[George W. Bush]] speak to reporters in May 2008]]

===Early career===
Tuberville first coached at [[Hermitage High School (Arkansas)|Hermitage High School]] in [[Hermitage, Arkansas]].<ref name=":3" /> He was an assistant coach at [[Arkansas State University]].<ref name=":3" /> He then went through the ranks at the [[University of Miami]], beginning as [[graduate assistant]] and ending as [[defensive coordinator]] in 1993, winning the national championship three times during his tenure there (1986–1994).<ref>{{Cite web |date=October 10, 2014 |title=Tommy Tuberville on Miami's glory years and current struggles |first=David M. |last=Hale |url=https://www.espn.com/blog/acc/post/_/id/75657/tommy-tuberville-on-miamis-glory-years-and-current-struggles |access-date=December 9, 2020 |website=ESPN.com |language=en |archive-date=March 10, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210310204811/https://www.espn.com/blog/acc/post/_/id/75657/tommy-tuberville-on-miamis-glory-years-and-current-struggles |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=UC hires Tommy Tuberville as new football coach|url=https://magazine.uc.edu/editors_picks/recent_features/tuberville_hired.html|access-date=December 9, 2020|website=University of Cincinnati|language=en-US|archive-date=January 8, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210108003633/https://magazine.uc.edu/editors_picks/recent_features/tuberville_hired.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In 1994, Tuberville replaced [[Bob Davie (American football)|Bob Davie]] as defensive coordinator under [[R. C. Slocum]] at [[Texas A&M University]]. The [[Texas A&M Aggies football|Aggies]] went 10–0–1 that season.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Tommy Tuberville - Football Coach|url=https://texastech.com/sports/football/roster/coaches/tommy-tuberville/227|access-date=December 9, 2020|website=Texas Tech Red Raiders|language=en|archive-date=March 10, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210310204323/https://texastech.com/sports/football/roster/coaches/tommy-tuberville/227|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Ole Miss===
Tuberville got his first collegiate head coaching job in 1994 at the [[University of Mississippi]] ("Ole Miss"). Despite taking over a [[Ole Miss Rebels football|Rebels]] team under severe [[National Collegiate Athletic Association|NCAA]] scholarship sanctions, he was named the [[Southeastern Conference football individual awards#Coach of the Year|SEC Coach of the Year]] in 1997 by the [[Associated Press|AP]].

At Ole Miss, Tuberville became involved in the movement to ban Confederate flags from the football stadium by requesting that the students quit waving them during the home football games.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.magnoliastatelive.com/2017/12/11/tommy-tuberville-power-compromise-helped-remove-confederate-flags-university-mississippi/ |title=How Tommy Tuberville and the power of compromise helped remove Confederate flags from the University of Mississippi |first=Alex |last=McDaniel |date=December 11, 2017|work=Magnolia State Live|access-date=March 7, 2020|language=en-US}}</ref> He said, "We can't recruit against the Confederate flag."<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.al.com/entertainment/2013/10/former_ole_miss_chancellor_tal.html |first=Bob |last=Carlton |title=Former Ole Miss chancellor talks about how Confederate flag ban changed a culture |date=October 29, 2013 |website=AL.com |language=en |access-date=March 15, 2020 |archive-date=March 8, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210308045008/https://www.al.com/entertainment/2013/10/former_ole_miss_chancellor_tal.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Ole Miss's chancellor, [[Robert Khayat]], ultimately placed a ban on sticks at football games, which effectively banned spectators from waving flags.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sharp |first1=John |title='An instrumental part': Did Tommy Tuberville get the Confederate flag removed from Ole Miss? |url=https://www.al.com/news/2020/06/an-instrumental-part-did-tommy-tuberville-get-the-confederate-flag-removed-from-ole-miss.html |access-date=July 29, 2020 |website=AL.com |date=June 13, 2020 |archive-date=December 17, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201217231842/https://www.al.com/news/2020/06/an-instrumental-part-did-tommy-tuberville-get-the-confederate-flag-removed-from-ole-miss.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

During his tenure, Tuberville was known as the "Riverboat Gambler" for his aggressive play-calling, especially on fourth down. His teams went 1–3 against [[Arkansas Razorbacks football|Arkansas]] and 2–2 against in-state arch-rival [[Mississippi State Bulldogs football|Mississippi State]] in the annual Egg Bowl game. After the 1998 regular season ended, Tuberville said, "They'll have to carry me out of here in a pine box", in reference to not leaving to coach at another school. Less than a week later, it was announced that he was departing for Auburn.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|title=Ole Miss won't forget old 'pine box' comments|url=http://archive.decaturdaily.com/decaturdaily/sports/columns/071026c.shtml|last=Dellenger|first=Ross|date=October 26, 2007|website=archive.decaturdaily.com|access-date=May 16, 2020|archive-date=November 6, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201106020711/http://archive.decaturdaily.com/decaturdaily/sports/columns/071026c.shtml|url-status=dead}}</ref>

===Auburn===
[[File:Tuberville-PluggingIn.jpg|thumb|right|Tuberville before the 2007 Vanderbilt game, his 100th career win]]
Tuberville left Ole Miss after the 1998 season to take the head coaching job at [[Auburn University]] in Alabama. At Auburn, he guided the [[Auburn Tigers football|Tigers]] to the top of the SEC standings, leading them to an SEC championship and the Western Division title in 2004. Under his direction, the Tigers made eight consecutive bowl appearances, including five New Year's Day bowl berths.

During the 1999 off-season, wide receiver Clifton Robinson was charged with statutory rape of a 15-year-old girl. Tuberville suspended Robinson from the team from March to August, when Robinson pleaded guilty to a misdemeanor charge of contributing to the delinquency of a minor and was sentenced to 200 hours of community service. Tuberville subsequently punished Robinson by suspending him for the season opener, then allowed him to rejoin the team.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lyman |first1=Brian |title=Tuberville campaign responds to report on former coach's handling of charges against player |url=https://www.montgomeryadvertiser.com/story/news/2020/07/02/tommy-tuberville-campaign-responds-report-former-coachs-handling-charges-against-player/5369933002/ |access-date=July 29, 2020 |publisher=Montgomery Advertiser |date=July 1, 2020 |archive-date=November 28, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201128121055/https://www.montgomeryadvertiser.com/story/news/2020/07/02/tommy-tuberville-campaign-responds-report-former-coachs-handling-charges-against-player/5369933002/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In 2004, Auburn went 13–0, including the SEC title and a win over [[2004 Virginia Tech Hokies football team|Virginia Tech]] in the [[2005 Sugar Bowl|Sugar Bowl]]. Nine selectors, including six of the Massey Index selectors, named Auburn as the national champions. Additionally, Auburn was the highest-ranked NCAA eligible team in final AP and Coaches polls after USC's bowl win was vacated. Tuberville was named college football coach of the year by the Associated Press, the American Football Coaches Association, the National Sportscasters and Sportswriters Association, and the Walter Camp Football Foundation.

In 2005, despite losing the entire starting backfield from the unbeaten 2004 team to the first round of the [[2005 NFL Draft|NFL draft]], Tuberville led Auburn to a 9–3 record, finishing the regular season with victories over rivals [[2005 Georgia Bulldogs football team|Georgia]] and [[2005 Alabama Crimson Tide football team|Alabama]].

Under Tuberville, Auburn had a winning record against its biggest rival, [[Alabama Crimson Tide football|Alabama]] (7–3), and was tied with its next two most significant rivals, [[Georgia Bulldogs football|Georgia]] (5–5) and [[LSU Tigers football|LSU]] (5–5). He was also 5–5 against [[Arkansas Razorbacks football|Arkansas]]. He led Auburn to six straight victories over in-state rival Alabama, the longest win streak in this rivalry since 1982, the year Auburn broke Alabama's nine-year winning streak.

Tuberville established himself as one of the best big-game coaches in college football, winning 9 of his last 15 games against top-10 opponents since the start of the 2004 season. In 2006, his Tigers beat two top-5 teams that later played in [[Bowl Championship Series|BCS]] bowls, including eventual BCS Champion [[2006 Florida Gators football team|Florida]]. Tuberville had a 5–2 career record against top-5 teams, including three wins against [[Florida Gators football|Florida]]. But he developed a reputation for losing games when he clearly had the better team. Examples include a humbling 24-point loss to a 4–5 [[2001 Alabama Crimson Tide football team|Alabama]] team in 2001 and a loss to [[2008 Vanderbilt Commodores football team|Vanderbilt]]&mdash;the first time Auburn lost to the Commodores in over 50 years. After Auburn lost three straight SEC games in 2003, Auburn booster [[Bobby Lowder]] and Auburn president and athletic director contacted then [[University of Louisville|Louisville]] head coach [[Bobby Petrino]] to gauge his interest in taking the Auburn job if Tuberville was fired. The press found out about the meeting, which occurred just before the 2003 [[2003 Alabama Crimson Tide football team|Alabama]] game, and the episode has since been known as "JetGate".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Tuberville on Bobby Petrino, JetGate, pine box quote |url=https://www.al.com/auburnfootball/2019/05/tommy-tuberville-on-bobby-petrino-jetgate-pine-box-quote-and-leaving-recruits-at-a-restaurant.html |date=May 23, 2019 |website=AL.com |language=en |access-date=May 16, 2020 |archive-date=December 14, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201214052658/https://www.al.com/auburnfootball/2019/05/tommy-tuberville-on-bobby-petrino-jetgate-pine-box-quote-and-leaving-recruits-at-a-restaurant.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Jetgate: A Look Back |url=https://www.collegeandmagnolia.com/2015/9/3/9251791/jetgate-a-look-back |date=September 3, 2015 |website=College and Magnolia |language=en |access-date=May 16, 2020 |archive-date=December 17, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201217190934/https://www.collegeandmagnolia.com/2015/9/3/9251791/jetgate-a-look-back |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=JetGate changed future of Louisville, Auburn |url=https://www.courier-journal.com/story/sports/college/louisville/2015/09/03/jetgate-bobby-petrino-tommy-tuberville-changed-future-louisville-football-auburn/71639212/ |last=Greer |first=Jeff |website=The Courier-Journal |language=en |access-date=May 16, 2020 |archive-date=March 10, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210310204811/https://www.courier-journal.com/story/sports/college/louisville/2015/09/03/jetgate-bobby-petrino-tommy-tuberville-changed-future-louisville-football-auburn/71639212/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Tuberville coached 19 players who were selected in the NFL draft, including four first-round picks in 2004, with several others signing as free agents. He coached eight All-Americans and a [[Thorpe Award]] winner ([[Carlos Rogers (American football)|Carlos Rogers]]). Thirty-four players under Tuberville were named to All-SEC (First Team). Eighteen were named All-SEC freshman. His players were named SEC player of the week 46 times. He also had two SEC players of the year and one SEC Championship game MVP.

Tuberville fired offensive coordinator [[Tony Franklin (coach)|Tony Franklin]] on October 8, 2008. After the 2008 season, with a 5–7 record including losses to [[2008 Vanderbilt Commodores football team|Vanderbilt]], [[2008 West Virginia Mountaineers football team|West Virginia]], and a final 36–0 loss to [[2008 Alabama Crimson Tide football team|Alabama]], Tuberville resigned.<ref>{{cite web|date=December 3, 2008|title=Tommy Tuberville Resigns As Head Football Coach At Auburn|url=http://www.auburntigers.com/sports/m-footbl/spec-rel/120308aaa.html|access-date=December 8, 2012|work=AuburnTigers.com|archive-date=March 12, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180312084143/http://www.auburntigers.com/sports/m-footbl/spec-rel/120308aaa.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> Auburn athletic director [[Jay Jacobs (athletics director)|Jay Jacobs]] said, "To say the least, I was a little shocked. But after three times of asking him would he change his mind, he convinced me that the best thing for him and his family and for this football program was for him to possibly take a year off and take a step back."<ref>{{Cite web|date=December 5, 2008|title=AD Says He Wanted Tuberville to Remain|url=https://www.tampabay.com/archive/2008/12/05/ad-says-he-wanted-tuberville-to-remain/|access-date=October 21, 2020|website=Tampa Bay Times|language=en|archive-date=October 27, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201027183119/https://www.tampabay.com/archive/2008/12/05/ad-says-he-wanted-tuberville-to-remain/|url-status=live}}</ref> With his departure, Tuberville was paid a prorated buyout of $5.1 million. The payments included $3 million within 30 days of his resignation date and the remainder within a year.

After his departure from Auburn, during the [[2009 NCAA Division I FBS football season|2009 football season]], Tuberville worked as an analyst for Buster Sports and ESPN, discussing the SEC and the Top 25 on various television shows and podcasts.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.al.com/goldmine/2009/09/blue_plate_special_tuberville.html |title=Blue Plate Special: Tuberville on Auburn's opener |work=blog.al.com |first=Charles |last=Goldberg |date=September 5, 2009 |access-date=October 12, 2010 |archive-date=November 17, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201117150803/https://www.al.com/goldmine/2009/09/blue_plate_special_tuberville.html |url-status=live }}</ref> He also made a cameo appearance in the [[Academy Awards|Academy Award]]-winning feature film ''[[The Blind Side (film)|The Blind Side]]''.

===Texas Tech===
[[File:Tommy Tuberville 2011 Texas Tech Red Raiders Spring Game.jpg|thumb|right|Tuberville during the 2011 Texas Tech Red Raiders Spring Game]]

On December 31, 2009, Tuberville expressed interest in becoming head coach of the [[Texas Tech Red Raiders football|Texas Tech Red Raiders]]. The position was left open after the university fired [[Mike Leach (American football coach)|Mike Leach]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.espn.com/college-football/news/story?id=4785127 |title=Ex-Auburn coach Tommy Tuberville expresses interest in Texas Tech Red Raiders job - ESPN |publisher=[[ESPN]] |date=December 31, 2009 |access-date=October 12, 2010 |archive-date=December 4, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201204083942/https://www.espn.com/college-football/news/story?id=4785127 |url-status=live }}</ref> On January 9, 2010, Tuberville was named head coach, and he was introduced at a press conference the next day.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.espn.com/college-football/news/story?id=4811310 |title=Tuberville named to coach Red Raiders |work=ESPN |date=January 9, 2010 |access-date=November 4, 2020 |archive-date=November 18, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201118023226/https://www.espn.com/college-football/news/story?id=4811310 |url-status=live }}</ref> On January 1, 2011, he became the second head coach in Texas Tech football history to win a bowl game in his first season—an accomplishment unmatched since [[DeWitt Weaver]]'s first season in 1951–52.<ref>{{cite news |first=C. W. "Pete" |last=Harland |date=March 20, 2011 |url=http://lubbockonline.com/editorials-letters/2011-03-20/letter-tuberville-wasnt-first-coach-win-bowl-first-tech-year |title=Tuberville wasn't first coach to win bowl in first Tech year |newspaper=Lubbock Avalanche-Journal |page=A12 |access-date=March 27, 2011 |archive-date=May 11, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180511081211/http://www.lubbockonline.com/editorials-letters/2011-03-20/letter-tuberville-wasnt-first-coach-win-bowl-first-tech-year |url-status=live }}</ref> This was a 45–38 victory over [[2010 Northwestern Wildcats football team|Northwestern]] in the [[2011 TicketCity Bowl|inaugural TicketCity Bowl]].

On January 18, 2011, Texas Tech announced that Tuberville received a one-year contract extension and a $500,000 per year raise. The extension and raise gave Tuberville a $2 million salary through the 2015 season. Tuberville was responsible for the highest-rated recruiting class in Texas Tech history, securing the 18th-ranked recruiting class in 2011, according to [[Rivals.com]] and the 14th-ranked class in the country according to [[Scout.com]].

On November 10, 2012, during a game against [[2012 Kansas Jayhawks football team|Kansas]], Tuberville yanked the hat and headset off his graduate assistant Kevin Oliver.<ref>{{cite news|last=Myerberg|first=Paul|title=Tommy Tuberville gets physical with an assistant (GIF)|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/gameon/2012/11/10/tuberville-violent-sidelines-neal-brown/1696529/|newspaper=USA Today|access-date=November 11, 2012|date=November 10, 2012|archive-date=August 4, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200804141954/https://www.usatoday.com/story/gameon/2012/11/10/tuberville-violent-sidelines-neal-brown/1696529/|url-status=live}}</ref> Immediately after the game, Tuberville said he was aiming for Oliver's shirt in an attempt to pull him off the field.<ref name="yahoo">{{cite web|title=Tommy Tuberville says he was just trying to get assistant off the field when he yanked off his headset|url=https://sports.yahoo.com/blogs/ncaaf-dr-saturday/tommy-tuberville-says-just-trying-assistant-off-field-224712309--ncaaf.html;_ylt=AsIR6fFxiHZKRfyf4DwyuS1UYsp_;_ylu=X3oDMTFkNWJ1MDBuBG1pdANCbG9nIEJvZHkEcG9zAzMEc2VjA01lZGlhQmxvZ0JvZHlBc3NlbWJseQ--;_ylg=X3oDMTJ0OTVmYWxiBGludGwDdXMEbGFuZwNlbi11cwRwc3RhaWQDMDRkYmIzMGMtNTY1Mi0zYWUzLWE2NTUtNjNjNGFlMTAyYTEwBHBzdGNhdANuY2FhZnxibG9nBHB0A3N0b3J5cGFnZQ--;_ylv=3|last=Schwab|first=Frank|date=November 10, 2012|website=Yahoo Sports|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141006104417/https://sports.yahoo.com/blogs/ncaaf-dr-saturday/tommy-tuberville-says-just-trying-assistant-off-field-224712309--ncaaf.html;_ylt=AsIR6fFxiHZKRfyf4DwyuS1UYsp_;_ylu=X3oDMTFkNWJ1MDBuBG1pdANCbG9nIEJvZHkEcG9zAzMEc2VjA01lZGlhQmxvZ0JvZHlBc3NlbWJseQ--;_ylg=X3oDMTJ0OTVmYWxiBGludGwDdXMEbGFuZwNlbi11cwRwc3RhaWQDMDRkYmIzMGMtNTY1Mi0zYWUzLWE2NTUtNjNjNGFlMTAyYTEwBHBzdGNhdANuY2FhZnxibG9nBHB0A3N0b3J5cGFnZQ--;_ylv=3|archive-date=October 6, 2014|access-date=December 14, 2012}}</ref> Two days later, he apologized in his weekly press conference, saying he wanted to set a better example for his two sons, one of whom was on the team.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2012-11-12 |title=Tommy Tuberville says he didn't slap assistant, but apologized for actions: 'You don't do things like that' |url=https://sports.yahoo.com/blogs/ncaaf-dr-saturday/tommy-tuberville-says-didn-t-slap-assistant-apologized-193208258--ncaaf.html |access-date=2023-08-01 |website=Yahoo Sports |language=en-US |archive-date=August 1, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230801170539/https://sports.yahoo.com/blogs/ncaaf-dr-saturday/tommy-tuberville-says-didn-t-slap-assistant-apologized-193208258--ncaaf.html |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Big 12 Conference|Big 12]] commissioner [[Bob Bowlsby]] publicly reprimanded Tuberville, calling his act "unsportsmanlike".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Big 12 reprimands Texas Tech coach for sideline altercation |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/gameon/2012/11/12/big-12-reviewing-tuberville-sideline-altercation/1700201/ |access-date=2023-08-01 |website=USA TODAY |language=en-US |archive-date=August 1, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230801165721/https://www.usatoday.com/story/gameon/2012/11/12/big-12-reviewing-tuberville-sideline-altercation/1700201/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Although Tuberville continued to run Leach's wide-open "Air Raid" [[spread offense]], he was never really embraced by a fan base still smarting over Leach's ouster.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/8741465/kliff-kingsbury-hired-texas-tech-red-raiders-coach | title=Texas Tech hires Kliff Kingsbury | first=Joe | last=Schad | work=ESPN | date=December 12, 2012 | access-date=November 4, 2020 | archive-date=October 29, 2013 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131029222017/http://espn.go.com/college-football/story/_/id/8741465/kliff-kingsbury-hired-texas-tech-red-raiders-coach | url-status=live }}</ref> According to a student on a recruiting trip to Texas Tech, Tuberville departed a recruiting dinner mid-meal and the next day accepted an offer to become Cincinnati's head coach.<ref>{{Cite news | title=Tommy Tuberville left recruits at dinner to take Cincinnati job | url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/gameon/2012/12/12/tommy-tuberville-texas-tech-cincinnati/1763615/ | last=Myerberg | first=Paul | date=December 12, 2012 | work=USA Today | language=en-US | access-date=May 16, 2020 | archive-date=January 28, 2021 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210128175443/https://www.usatoday.com/story/gameon/2012/12/12/tommy-tuberville-texas-tech-cincinnati/1763615/ | url-status=live }}</ref> He left Texas Tech with an overall record of 20–17 and 9–17 in Big 12 conference play.

In 2025, Tuberville said he recruited [[Patrick Mahomes]], who played at Texas Tech from 2014 to 2016, but Mahomes said that was not true.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Chiefs' Patrick Mahomes clarifies his relationship with U.S. Sen. Tommy Tuberville: 'He did not recruit me'|url=https://www.cbssports.com/nfl/news/chiefs-patrick-mahomes-clarifies-his-relationship-with-u-s-sen-tommy-tuberville-he-did-not-recruit-me/|date=2025-02-06|website=CBS|first=Steven|last=Taranto|access-date=2025-06-27|archive-date=February 7, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250207132440/https://www.cbssports.com/nfl/news/chiefs-patrick-mahomes-clarifies-his-relationship-with-u-s-sen-tommy-tuberville-he-did-not-recruit-me/|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Cincinnati===
On December 8, 2012, Tuberville resigned as head coach at Texas Tech in order to become the 38th head coach at the [[University of Cincinnati]]. He signed a $2.2 million contract to coach the team.<ref name=CE>{{cite news |last=Peale |first=Cliff |title=Cincinnati's Tommy Tuberville will make $2.2M |url=http://news.cincinnati.com/article/20130102/SPT0101/301020144/UC-s-Tuberville-will-make-least-1-6m?nclick_check=1 |access-date=January 3, 2013 |newspaper=[[The Cincinnati Enquirer]] |date=January 2, 2013 |archive-date=March 10, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210310204955/https://www.cincinnati.com/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://lubbockonline.com/filed-online/2012-12-08/tuberville-leaves-texas-tech-cincinnati |title=Tuberville leaves Texas Tech for Cincinnati |last=Kosmider |first=Nick |date=December 8, 2012 |work=[[Lubbock Avalanche-Journal]] |access-date=December 8, 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121210162329/http://lubbockonline.com/sports-red-raiders-football/2012-12-08/tuberville-leaves-texas-tech-cincinnati|archive-date=December 10, 2012 |url-status=dead}}</ref> Cincinnati's athletic director, Whit Babcock, had previously worked with Tuberville at Auburn; the two had been friends for several years.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://apnews.com/3e41688147be43e5897dda92a9ffc678 |work=Associated Press |first1=Joe |last1=Kayap |first2=Betsy |last2=Blaney |title=Cincinnati hires Tuberville as football coach |date=December 8, 2012 |access-date=July 24, 2023 |archive-date=July 24, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230724113921/https://apnews.com/3e41688147be43e5897dda92a9ffc678 |url-status=live }}</ref> On December 9, a ''[[Lubbock Avalanche-Journal]]'' article pointed out that Cincinnati is only {{convert|30|mi|km|-1}} from [[Guilford, Indiana]], home of Tuberville's wife, Suzanne.<ref name="Kosmidier A1, A9">{{cite news |url=https://eu.lubbockonline.com/story/sports/college/football/2012/12/08/tuberville-leaves-texas-tech-cincinnati/15116738007/ |title=Cincinnati, really?: Tuberville bolts Lubbock; stuns community, team |newspaper=Lubbock Avalanche-Journal |pages=A1, A9 |access-date=December 13, 2012 |date=December 9, 2012 |first=Nick |last=Kosmidier |archive-date=November 20, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241120124245/https://eu.lubbockonline.com/story/sports/college/football/2012/12/08/tuberville-leaves-texas-tech-cincinnati/15116738007/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In 2013, his first season with Cincinnati, Tuberville led the Bearcats to an overall record of 9–4 and a 6–2 conference record.<ref name="grfx"/> His 2014 team was also 9–4 overall, but this time earned an American Athletic Conference co-championship by virtue of their 7–1 league mark.<ref>{{cite web |title=Bearcats earn share of AAC title with win over Houston |url=https://www.cincinnati.com/story/sports/college/university-of-cincinnati/2014/12/06/cincinnati-bearcats-football-houston-cougars/19972513/ |last=Groeschen |first=Tom |date=December 6, 2014 |website=The Enquirer |publisher=USA Today |access-date=August 14, 2025 |archive-date=July 1, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220701023321/https://www.cincinnati.com/story/sports/college/university-of-cincinnati/2014/12/06/cincinnati-bearcats-football-houston-cougars/19972513/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Both years also saw bowl losses, in 2013 to [[2013 North Carolina Tar Heels football team|North Carolina]] and 2014 to [[2014 Virginia Tech Hokies football team|Virginia Tech]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.downthedrive.com/2014/12/27/7455371/bearcats-drop-military-bowl-to-virginia-tech |title=Bearcats Drop Military Bowl To Virginia Tech |first=Matt |last=Opper |date=December 27, 2014 |website=Down The Drive |access-date=January 8, 2021 |archive-date=November 11, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201111050443/https://www.downthedrive.com/2014/12/27/7455371/bearcats-drop-military-bowl-to-virginia-tech |url-status=live }}</ref>

On December 4, 2016, after a 4–8 season, Tuberville resigned as head coach of Cincinnati.<ref>{{cite web |title=Tommy Tuberville steps down at Cincinnati after 4-8 season |url=https://www.cbssports.com/college-football/news/tommy-tuberville-steps-down-at-cincinnati-after-4-8-season/ |last1=Chip |first1=Patterson |last2=Dodd |first2=Dennis |date=December 4, 2016 |website=CBS Sports |access-date=August 14, 2025 |archive-date=March 5, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210305212319/https://www.cbssports.com/college-football/news/tommy-tuberville-steps-down-at-cincinnati-after-4-8-season/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He left Cincinnati with an overall record of 29–22 and 18–14 in AAC conference play.

== TS Capital ==
After resigning from Auburn in December 2008, Tuberville formed a 50-50 partnership with former Lehman Brothers broker John David Stroud, creating TS Capital Management and TS Capital Partners,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hakim |first=Danny |date=October 12, 2020 |title=A Trump-Backed Senate Candidate's Hedge Fund Disaster |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/07/05/us/politics/tommy-tuberville-senate-hedge-fund.html |access-date=October 21, 2020 |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=February 27, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210227140828/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/07/05/us/politics/tommy-tuberville-senate-hedge-fund.html |url-status=live }}</ref> where he had an office and helped find investors.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Goldberg |first=Charles |date=July 26, 2009 |title=Tommy Tuberville keeps eye on coaching future while enjoying time off and memories of Auburn |url=https://www.al.com/goldmine/2009/07/_auburn_tommy_tuberville.html |access-date=October 21, 2020 |website=AL.com |publisher=Birmingham News |language=en |archive-date=December 14, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201214052657/https://www.al.com/goldmine/2009/07/_auburn_tommy_tuberville.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In February 2012, seven investors sued Tuberville and Stroud, saying they were defrauded of more than $1.7 million<ref>{{Cite web |last=Turner |first=John |date=February 28, 2012 |title=Former Auburn coach Tommy Tuberville subject of $1.7 million fraud lawsuit (updated) |url=https://www.al.com/breaking/2012/02/former_auburn_coach_tommy_tube.html |access-date=May 16, 2020 |website=The Huntsville Times |language=en |archive-date=November 27, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201127192058/https://www.al.com/breaking/2012/02/former_auburn_coach_tommy_tube.html |url-status=live }}</ref> that they invested from 2008 to 2011.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Anthony |first=Chris |date=February 28, 2012 |title=Tuberville sued over alleged investment scheme |url=https://oanow.com/news/tuberville-sued-over-alleged-investment-scheme/article_c9993bba-2cbb-5c72-b425-b44e7c140598.html |access-date=October 21, 2020 |website=Opelika-Auburn News |language=en |archive-date=November 3, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201103080009/https://oanow.com/news/tuberville-sued-over-alleged-investment-scheme/article_c9993bba-2cbb-5c72-b425-b44e7c140598.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Tuberville's attorneys denied the allegations.<ref>{{Cite web |date=February 28, 2012 |title=Tuberville responds to fraud lawsuit |url=https://www.kcbd.com/story/17037439/update-tuberville-responds-to-fraud-lawsuit/ |access-date=October 21, 2020 |website=[[KCBD]] |language=en-US |archive-date=October 31, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201031151417/https://www.kcbd.com/story/17037439/update-tuberville-responds-to-fraud-lawsuit/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

In May 2012, Stroud was indicted for fraudulent use of $5.2 million from various Auburn investment companies, including his partnerships with Tuberville;<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|last=Taylor|first=Drew|date=November 8, 2013|title=Former Auburn businessman sentenced to 10 years in prison for investment fraud|url=https://oanow.com/news/crime_courts/former-auburn-businessman-sentenced-to-10-years-in-prison-for-investment-fraud/article_b3823a4c-50b8-11e3-9b52-001a4bcf6878.html|access-date=October 21, 2020|website=Opelika-Auburn News|language=en|archive-date=March 10, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210310204304/https://oanow.com/news/crime_courts/former-auburn-businessman-sentenced-to-10-years-in-prison-for-investment-fraud/article_b3823a4c-50b8-11e3-9b52-001a4bcf6878.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Tuberville was not charged.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Cason|first=Mike|date=October 13, 2020|title=Tax records not clear on Tuberville charity's spending|url=https://www.al.com/news/2020/10/tax-records-not-clear-on-tuberville-veterans-charitys-spending.html|access-date=October 21, 2020|website=AL.com|language=en|archive-date=January 8, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210108002706/https://www.al.com/news/2020/10/tax-records-not-clear-on-tuberville-veterans-charitys-spending.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Tuberville said in court filings that he was also a victim, and had lost $450,000; he settled the investor lawsuit in October 2013 on undisclosed terms.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Watkins|first=Steve|date=October 10, 2013|title=UC coach Tuberville settles investment lawsuit|url=https://www.bizjournals.com/cincinnati/news/2013/10/10/uc-coach-tuberville-settles-investment.html|access-date=October 21, 2020|website=bizjournals.com|archive-date=November 25, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201125024114/https://www.bizjournals.com/cincinnati/news/2013/10/10/uc-coach-tuberville-settles-investment.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=October 10, 2013 |title=Cincinnati's Tommy Tuberville settles fraud lawsuit |work=USA Today |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/ncaaf/2013/10/10/cincinnatis-tommy-tuberville-settles-fraud-lawsuit/2960837/ |access-date=July 24, 2023 |archive-date=January 27, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210127034021/https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/ncaaf/2013/10/10/cincinnatis-tommy-tuberville-settles-fraud-lawsuit/2960837/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In November 2013, Stroud pleaded guilty and received a 10-year sentence.<ref name=":0" />

==Tommy Tuberville Foundation==
In 2014, Tuberville founded the Tommy Tuberville Foundation. Its website said its purpose was "to recognize and support organizations and causes that connect with the beliefs and values of the Tuberville family: assisting our military and veterans; awareness, education and prevention of health issues, particularly among women and children; and, education and community initiatives."<ref>{{Cite web |date=October 31, 2020 |title=Official Website of the Tommy Tuberville Foundation |url=http://www.tommytubervillefoundation.org/ |access-date=April 12, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201031173224/http://www.tommytubervillefoundation.org/ |archive-date=October 31, 2020 }}</ref>

Through its first five years, the foundation raised $289,599 but spent just $51,658 on charitable causes, tax records showed.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Burkhalter |first=Eddie |date=November 2, 2020 |title=Tuberville's charity for veterans spent little on vets, tax records show |url=https://www.alreporter.com/2020/11/02/tommy-tubervilles-charity-for-veterans-spent-little-on-vets-tax-records-show/ |access-date=April 12, 2023 |website=Alabama Political Reporter |language=en-US |archive-date=April 12, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230412035455/https://www.alreporter.com/2020/11/02/tommy-tubervilles-charity-for-veterans-spent-little-on-vets-tax-records-show/ |url-status=live }}</ref> This rate of 18% is less than the 65% that the [[Better Business Bureau]] says ethical charities should spend on their causes.<ref>{{Cite web |date=August 23, 2020 |title=Standards For Charity Accountability |url=https://www.bbb.org/us/storage/0/shared%20documents/standards%20for%20charity%20accountability.pdf |access-date=April 12, 2023 |website=Better Business Bureau|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200823131540/https://www.bbb.org/us/storage/0/shared%20documents/standards%20for%20charity%20accountability.pdf |archive-date=August 23, 2020 }}</ref> In 2020, the [[Associated Press]] called the Tuberville Foundation "a questionable charity that raises money but gives very little away".<ref name="ap">{{cite news |last=Chandler |first=Kim |date=October 12, 2020 |title=Tuberville dealings include failed hedge fund, charity |work=[[Associated Press]] |url=https://apnews.com/article/election-2020-senate-elections-golf-lawsuits-football-9ca5887bba8ec5778f07694a6dc25bc4 |access-date=October 12, 2020 |archive-date=January 6, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210106084201/https://apnews.com/article/election-2020-senate-elections-golf-lawsuits-football-9ca5887bba8ec5778f07694a6dc25bc4 |url-status=live }}</ref> Foundation officials said the tax filings did not reflect volunteer labor and donated materials used to refurbish veterans' homes.<ref name="whnt">{{cite news |last=Fitzgerald |first=Ethan |date=October 12, 2020 |title=IRS documents show Tuberville Foundation kept money from vets |url=https://whnt.com/news/politics/your-local-election-headquarters/irs-documents-show-tuberville-foundation-kept-money-from-vets/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230907162434/https://whnt.com/news/politics/your-local-election-headquarters/irs-documents-show-tuberville-foundation-kept-money-from-vets/ |url-status=live |archive-date=2023-09-07 |agency=[[Associated Press]] |access-date=October 12, 2020}}</ref>

In 2020, ''The New York Times'' reported that Tuberville campaign and foundation officials "produced internal records for 2018 that showed nearly $20,000 was raised for a temporary project to provide a retreat for veterans. But the records raised bookkeeping questions, since they showed more than $61,000 of 2018 revenue, roughly twice what the charity reported to the [[Internal Revenue Service|I.R.S.]] that year".<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hakim |first=Danny |date=October 12, 2020 |title=Tommy Tuberville's Financial Fumbles |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/10/12/us/elections/senate-alabama-tuberville-jones.html |access-date=April 12, 2023 |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=February 10, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210210021814/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/10/12/us/elections/senate-alabama-tuberville-jones.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

In 2021, the ''Washington Post'' reported, the foundation "reported it had $74,101 in revenue and spent just 12 percent of that, or $9,000, while $32,000 went to administrative costs (including nearly $12,400 to pay off a truck the charity purchased in 2018 for $27,369)".<ref name=":4" /> By the end of 2021, the foundation's website had gone defunct.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Tommy Tuberville Foundation |url=http://www.tommytubervillefoundation.org/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211224173932/http://www.tommytubervillefoundation.org/ |archive-date=December 24, 2021 |website=Tommy Tuberville Foundation}}</ref>

In July 2023, a spokesperson for Tuberville said that the foundation had been under audit and had paused its activities, but that Tuberville was reforming it.<ref name=":4">{{cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/07/19/tommy-tuberville-veterans-fact-checker/ |title=Tommy Tuberville pledged to 'donate every dime' to veterans. He hasn't. |first=Glenn |last=Kessler |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=July 19, 2023 |access-date=July 23, 2023 |archive-date=July 20, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230720173501/https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/07/19/tommy-tuberville-veterans-fact-checker/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

== U.S. Senate ==
===Election===
{{Main|2020 United States Senate election in Alabama}}
[[File:Senator Doug Jones official photo.jpg|thumb|In the [[2020 United States Senate election in Alabama|2020 U.S. Senate race]], Tuberville defeated incumbent Sen. [[Doug Jones (politician)|Doug Jones]]]]
In August 2018, Tuberville moved from Florida to Alabama with the intention to run for the U.S. Senate in 2020.<ref>{{cite news |date=February 17, 2020 |title=Alabama Senate race turns nasty with 2 weeks to go |url=https://www.al.com/news/2020/02/alabama-senate-race-turns-nasty-with-2-weeks-to-go.html |work=AL.com |first=Paul |last=Gattis |location=Birmingham, AL |archive-date=November 8, 2020 |access-date=July 15, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108053444/https://www.al.com/news/2020/02/alabama-senate-race-turns-nasty-with-2-weeks-to-go.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In April 2019, he announced he would enter the [[2020 United States Senate election in Alabama#Republican primary|2020 Republican primary]] for the Senate seat held by Democrat [[Doug Jones (politician)|Doug Jones]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://apnews.com/daa8c8f590754916b027dd0e7256c8f1 |title=Ex-Auburn football coach Tuberville to run for Ala. Senate |last=Miller |first=Zeke |date=April 6, 2019 |website=Associated Press |access-date=April 6, 2019 |archive-date=November 11, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201111014213/https://apnews.com/daa8c8f590754916b027dd0e7256c8f1 |url-status=live }}</ref> Tuberville's campaign was described as "low-profile," with few pre-scheduled campaign appearances or press conferences.<ref name="defeats jeff sessions">{{cite web |title=Tommy Tuberville defeats Jeff Sessions in Alabama Republican Senate runoff |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/07/14/tommy-tuberville-defeats-jeff-sessions-alabama-gop-senate-runoff-results/5440395002/ |work=USA Today |first=Brian |last=Lyman |date=July 14, 2020 |access-date=July 24, 2023 |archive-date=November 14, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201114134357/https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/07/14/tommy-tuberville-defeats-jeff-sessions-alabama-gop-senate-runoff-results/5440395002/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He closely allied himself with President [[Donald Trump]].<ref name="defeats jeff sessions" /> Former White House press secretary [[Sean Spicer]] was a member of Tuberville's campaign staff.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.cincinnati.com/story/sports/college/university-of-cincinnati/2019/04/06/tommy-tuberville-run-u-s-senate-alabama-per-report/3387095002/ |title=Tommy Tuberville, former UC Bearcats coach, declares he'll run for U.S. Senate in Alabama |last=Clark |first=Dave |work=[[The Cincinnati Enquirer]] |date=April 6, 2019 |archive-date=August 4, 2020 |access-date=April 6, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200804171535/https://www.cincinnati.com/story/sports/college/university-of-cincinnati/2019/04/06/tommy-tuberville-run-u-s-senate-alabama-per-report/3387095002/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Tuberville opposes the right to an abortion and favors repealing the [[Affordable Care Act]] (Obamacare). He supports Trump's proposal to build a wall on the border with Mexico.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pateras |first=Grace |title=Alabama senate race 2020: Meet the candidates running for US Senate on Super Tuesday |date=2020-02-12 |url=https://www.montgomeryadvertiser.com/story/news/2020/02/12/alabama-primary-election-day-2020-senate-candidates-doug-jones-jeff-sessions/4723266002/ |access-date=May 24, 2020 |website=The Montgomery Advertiser |language=en |archive-date=January 18, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210118142852/https://www.montgomeryadvertiser.com/story/news/2020/02/12/alabama-primary-election-day-2020-senate-candidates-doug-jones-jeff-sessions/4723266002/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Tuberville supports reducing the [[national debt of the United States|national debt]] through cuts to social programs, but opposes cuts to [[Social Security (United States)|Social Security]], [[Medicare (United States)|Medicare]], or [[Medicaid]].<ref name="defeats jeff sessions" /> He dismisses the science of [[climate change]], saying that the global climate "won't change enough in the next 400 years to affect anybody."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bruggers |first=James |date=September 17, 2020 |title=Senate 2020: In Alabama, Two Very Different Views on Climate Change Give Voters a Clear Choice |url=https://insideclimatenews.org/news/16092020/senate-2020-alabama-climate-change-doug-jones-tommy-tuberville |access-date=September 17, 2020 |website=InsideClimate News |language=en-US |archive-date=November 22, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201122163204/https://insideclimatenews.org/news/16092020/senate-2020-alabama-climate-change-doug-jones-tommy-tuberville |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=October 31, 2019 |title=Tuberville talks on issues during DME interview |url=http://mountaineagle.com/stories/tuberville-talks-on-issues-during-dme-interview,22557 |url-status=live |access-date=September 17, 2020 |website=Daily Mountain Eagle |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191105003401/http://mountaineagle.com/stories/tuberville-talks-on-issues-during-dme-interview,22557 |first=Ed |last=Howell |archive-date=November 5, 2019 |language=en}}</ref>

On March 3, 2020, Tuberville received 33.4% of the vote in the Republican primary, ahead of former United States senator and former attorney general [[Jeff Sessions]], who received 31.6%. Because neither candidate won over 50% of the vote, a [[two-round system|runoff]] election ensued.

On March 10, ahead of the runoff election, Trump endorsed Tuberville.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Haberman |first1=Maggie |title=Trump Endorses Tommy Tuberville (and Not Jeff Sessions) for Senate in Alabama |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/10/us/politics/trump-tommy-tuberville-sessions.html |access-date=May 5, 2020 |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=March 10, 2020 |archive-date=February 20, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210220015018/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/03/10/us/politics/trump-tommy-tuberville-sessions.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Trump had been angered by Sessions's decision to [[recusal|recuse]] himself from the investigation into [[Russian interference in the 2016 United States elections]] when Sessions was U.S. attorney general.<ref name="defeats jeff sessions" /> In May 2020, Trump called Sessions "slime" for this decision.<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 23, 2020 |title=Trump calls Sessions 'slime,' urges him to exit Senate race |first=John |last=Sharp |url=https://www.al.com/politics/2020/05/jeff-sessions-trump-twitter-battle-spills-into-saturday.html |access-date=May 24, 2020 |website=AL.com |language=en |archive-date=January 17, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210117185911/https://www.al.com/politics/2020/05/jeff-sessions-trump-twitter-battle-spills-into-saturday.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In campaign ads, Tuberville attacked Sessions for not being "man enough to stand with President Trump when things got tough."<ref name="projected to lose"/> In the July 14 runoff, Tuberville defeated Sessions with 60.7% of the vote.<ref name="projected to lose">{{cite web |title=Jeff Sessions Is Projected To Lose Comeback Bid For Alabama Senate Seat |url=https://www.npr.org/2020/07/14/890782768/jeff-sessions-is-projected-to-lose-comeback-bid-for-alabama-senate-seat |first=Elena |last=Moore |work=NPR |date=July 14, 2020 |access-date=July 15, 2020 |archive-date=March 9, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210309220555/https://www.npr.org/2020/07/14/890782768/jeff-sessions-is-projected-to-lose-comeback-bid-for-alabama-senate-seat |url-status=live }}</ref>

As the Republican nominee, Tuberville was heavily favored to win the election.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Lyman|first1=Brian|date=October 5, 2020|title=Alabama US Senate poll: Tommy Tuberville has 12-point lead on Doug Jones|work=The Montgomery Advertiser|url=https://www.montgomeryadvertiser.com/story/news/2020/10/05/alabama-senate-poll-tommy-tuberville-has-12-point-lead-doug-jones/3629590001/|access-date=October 12, 2020|archive-date=October 20, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201020203313/https://www.montgomeryadvertiser.com/story/news/2020/10/05/alabama-senate-poll-tommy-tuberville-has-12-point-lead-doug-jones/3629590001/|url-status=live}}</ref> He was endorsed by the [[National Right to Life Committee]], America's largest [[anti-abortion]] organization.<ref>{{Cite web|date=July 28, 2020|title=America's Largest Anti-Abortion Group Endorses Tommy Tuberville|url=https://www.bamapolitics.com/56861/americas-largest-anti-abortion-group-endorses-tommy-tuberville/|first=Dakota|last=Layton|access-date=July 28, 2020|website=Bama Politics|language=en|archive-date=December 6, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201206014510/https://www.bamapolitics.com/56861/americas-largest-anti-abortion-group-endorses-tommy-tuberville/|url-status=live}}</ref> On November 3, he defeated Jones with 60.1% of the vote.<ref name=":2">{{Cite news |date=November 3, 2020 |title=Alabama U.S. Senate Election Results |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2020/11/03/us/elections/results-alabama-senate.html |access-date=November 4, 2020 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=March 7, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210307232123/https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2020/11/03/us/elections/results-alabama-senate.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

In an ''Alabama Daily News'' interview after the election, Tuberville said that the [[European theater of World War II]] was fought "to free Europe of socialism" and erroneously that the three branches of the [[Federal government of the United States|U.S. federal government]] were "the House, the Senate, and the executive." He also said that he was looking forward to raising money from his Senate office, a violation of federal law. Tuberville's comments attracted criticism.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/11/13/us/tommy-tuberville-fact-check.html|title=Senator-elect Tommy Tuberville flubs basics of the Constitution, World War II and the 2000 election.|first=Catie|last=Edmondson|date=November 13, 2020|newspaper=New York Times|access-date=November 14, 2020|archive-date=February 24, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210224185315/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/11/13/us/tommy-tuberville-fact-check.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://aldailynews.com/in-the-weeds-w-tommy-tuberville-alabamas-next-senator/ |first=Todd |last=Stacy |access-date=July 24, 2023 |title=In the Weeds w/ Tommy Tuberville, Alabama's next senator |work=Alabama Daily News |date=November 12, 2020 |archive-date=July 24, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230724085509/https://aldailynews.com/in-the-weeds-w-tommy-tuberville-alabamas-next-senator/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

On November 26, 2020, Tuberville announced that his chief of staff would be [[Stephen Boyd (attorney)|Stephen Boyd]], who had been serving as assistant attorney general for the Office of Legislative Affairs at the U.S. Department of Justice.<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 26, 2020 |title=Tuberville names Boyd as chief of staff |url=https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2020/nov/26/tuberville-names-boyd-as-chief-of-staff/ |access-date=July 28, 2023 |website=The Washington Times |language=en-US |archive-date=January 16, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210116223600/https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2020/nov/26/tuberville-names-boyd-as-chief-of-staff/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

