== 2026 gubernatorial campaign ==
{{Main|2026 Alabama gubernatorial election}}
On May 27, 2025, Tuberville announced that he would not seek a second term in the United States Senate and would instead run for [[governor of Alabama]].<ref>{{Cite web |last= Tynes |first= Gabriel |date= 2025-05-27 |title= Tuberville passes on Senate reelection to launch Alabama gubernatorial campaign |url= https://www.courthousenews.com/tuberville-passes-on-senate-re-election-to-launch-alabama-gubernatorial-campaign |access-date= 2026-07-09 |website= Courthouse News Service |language= en-us}}</ref> Facing no major challengers, Tuberville won the Republican nomination on May 19, 2026.<ref>{{Cite web |date= 2026-05-20 |title= Sen. Tommy Tuberville wins Republican primary for governor of Alabama |url= https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/alabama-governor-primary-election-winners-tommy-tuberville-rcna345002 |access-date= 2026-07-09 |website= NBC News |language= en}}</ref>

Tuberville has faced several [[2026 Alabama gubernatorial election#Challenges to Tuberville's eligibility|challenges to his eligibility for the gubernatorial office]], with opponents alleging that he does not satisfy a seven-year Alabama residency requirement set out in the [[Constitution of Alabama]].<ref>{{Cite web |last= Britt |first= Bill |date= 2026-06-23 |title= Analysis {{!}} Tuberville's residency case may test the Alabama Constitution |url= https://www.alreporter.com/2026/06/23/analysis-tubervilles-residency-case-may-test-the-alabama-constitution/ |access-date= 2026-07-09 |website= Alabama Political Reporter |language= en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= Lovell |first= Ryan |date= 2026-06-15 |title= WATCH: Alabama GOP clears Tuberville for governor race |url= https://www.wvtm13.com/article/alabama-gop-final-ruling-tommy-tuberville-eligibility-ken-mcfeeters/71581825 |access-date= 2026-07-09 |website= WVTM |language= en}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
[[File:Tommy Tuberville with son at Joe Biden 2021 presidential inauguration.jpg|thumb|Tuberville with his son Tucker at the [[inauguration of Joe Biden]]]]
Tuberville married Vicki Lynn Harris, also from Camden, Arkansas, and a graduate of Harmony Grove High School, on December 19, 1976.<ref>{{Cite web|title=30 Dec 1976, Page 5 - The Camden News at Newspapers.com|url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/34959440/?terms=Vicki+Harris|access-date=March 11, 2020|website=Newspapers.com|language=en|archive-date=March 10, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210310204249/http://www.newspapers.com/image/34959440/?terms=Vicki+Harris|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=11 Nov 1976, Page 6 - The Camden News at Newspapers.com|url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/34960136/?terms=Thomas+Hawley+Tuberville|access-date=March 11, 2020|website=Newspapers.com|language=en|archive-date=March 10, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210310205032/http://www.newspapers.com/image/34960136/?terms=Thomas+Hawley+Tuberville|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=27 Nov 1976, Page 3 - The Camden News at Newspapers.com|url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/34960461/?terms=Thomas+Hawley+Tuberville|access-date=March 11, 2020|website=Newspapers.com|language=en|archive-date=March 10, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210310204250/http://www.newspapers.com/image/34960461/?terms=Thomas+Hawley+Tuberville|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=19 Jan 1977, Page 9 - The Camden News at Newspapers.com|url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/31461350/?terms=Thomas+Hawley+Tuberville|access-date=March 11, 2020|website=Newspapers.com|language=en}}</ref> They later divorced. In 1991, Tuberville married Suzanne (née Fette) of [[Guilford, Indiana]]; they have two sons.<ref name="Kosmidier A1, A9" /> His elder son, Tucker, played as a quarterback while he was coach at [[Texas Tech Red Raiders football|Texas Tech]], before following him to [[Cincinnati Bearcats football|Cincinnati]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.al.com/auburnfootball/2013/07/with_coaching_aspirations_in_t.html|title=With coaching aspirations in tow, Tucker Tuberville transfers to Auburn to follow father's footsteps|website=AL.com|first=Brandon|last=Marcello|date=July 26, 2013|access-date=November 3, 2023}}</ref> Tucker then [[Walk-on (sports)|walked-on]] at [[Auburn Tigers football|Auburn]],<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.al.com/auburnfootball/2013/07/tommy_tubervilles_son_to_walk.html|title=Tommy Tuberville's son to walk on, play quarterback at Auburn|website=AL.com|first=Brandon|last=Marcello|date=July 22, 2013|access-date=November 3, 2023|archive-date=November 3, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231103010144/https://www.al.com/auburnfootball/2013/07/tommy_tubervilles_son_to_walk.html|url-status=live}}</ref> where he was used as a scout team quarterback during his three seasons there, and made his sole appearance for the team in the fourth quarter of a 56–34 win over [[2015 Idaho Vandals football team|Idaho]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://auburntigers.com/news/2015/11/22/it_s_a_day_to_remember_for_tucker_tuberville_and_his_dad.aspx|title=It's a day to remember for Tucker Tuberville and his dad|publisher=Auburn Tigers|first=Charles|last=Goldberg|work=Auburn University Athletics|date=November 22, 2015|access-date=November 3, 2023|archive-date=November 3, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231103011647/https://auburntigers.com/news/2015/11/22/it_s_a_day_to_remember_for_tucker_tuberville_and_his_dad.aspx|url-status=live}}</ref>

Tuberville has a brother, Charles.<ref name="brother"/>

Tuberville invested $1.9 million in GLC Enterprises, which the [[U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission|Securities and Exchange Commission]] called an $80 million [[Ponzi scheme]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=August 16, 2012 |title=SEC Charges College Football Hall of Fame Coach in $80 Million Ponzi Scheme |url=https://www.sec.gov/news/press-release/2012-2012-157htm |access-date=October 21, 2020 |website=[[U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission]] |archive-date=November 27, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201127023709/https://www.sec.gov/news/press-release/2012-2012-157htm |url-status=live }}</ref> He lost about $150,000 when the business closed in 2011.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hakim |first=Danny |date=October 12, 2020 |title=Tommy Tuberville's Financial Fumbles |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/10/12/us/elections/senate-alabama-tuberville-jones.html |access-date=October 21, 2020 |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=February 10, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210210021814/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/10/12/us/elections/senate-alabama-tuberville-jones.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

At Auburn, Tuberville participated in the Auburn Church of Christ.<ref>{{cite web |date=August 20, 1995 |title=Tuberville in ''Christian Chronicle'' |url=http://www.christianchronicle.org/article2098532~Across_the_nation |access-date=October 12, 2010 |publisher=Christianchronicle.org |archive-date=March 20, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120320075552/http://www.christianchronicle.org/article2098532~Across_the_nation |url-status=dead}}</ref>

Tuberville's interests include "[[NASCAR]], golf, football, hunting and fishing, [and] America's military". He enjoys [[country and western music]].<ref name="Ritz 28">{{Citation |last=Ritz |first=Jennifer |date=September–October 2010 |title=Tommy Tuberville |journal=Texas Techsan: The Magazine for Texas Tech Alumni |volume=63 |number=5 |page=28}}</ref> Tuberville is also a fan of Filipino boxer [[Manny Pacquiao]].<ref name="Ritz 28"/>

In August 2023, ''[[The Washington Post]]'' reported that campaign finance and property records indicate that Tuberville lives in [[Santa Rosa Beach, Florida]],<ref name= "Kessler">{{Cite news |last= Kessler |first= Glen |author-link= Glenn Kessler (journalist) |date= August 10, 2023 |title= Tommy Tuberville: Florida's third senator? |newspaper= The Washington Post |url= https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/08/10/tommy-tuberville-floridas-third-senator/ |access-date= August 10, 2023 |archive-date= August 10, 2023 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230810173141/https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2023/08/10/tommy-tuberville-floridas-third-senator/ |url-status= live }}</ref><ref name= "Labbate">{{Cite news |last= Labbate |first= Mariana |date= August 10, 2023 |title= Alabama Sen. Tommy Tuberville Actually Lives in Florida: Report |work= [[The Messenger (website)|The Messenger]] |url= https://themessenger.com/politics/alabama-sen-tommy-tuberville-actually-lives-in-florida-report |access-date= August 10, 2023 |archive-date= August 10, 2023 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230810194846/https://themessenger.com/politics/alabama-sen-tommy-tuberville-actually-lives-in-florida-report |url-status= dead }}</ref> instead of Auburn, Alabama, as his office claims,<ref>{{Cite web |date= 2023-07-28 |title= About » Coach Tommy Tuberville |url= https://www.tuberville.senate.gov/about/ |access-date= 2023-08-11 |archive-date= July 28, 2023 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230728175444/https://www.tuberville.senate.gov/about/ |url-status= live }}</ref> and has for almost two decades. Despite filing taxes as an Alabama resident in 2018, Tuberville voted in the Florida election that year.<ref>{{Cite news |last= Moon |first= Josh |date= 2026-06-03 |title= Analysis {{!}} The fight over Tommy Tuberville's residency status is officially under way |url= https://www.alreporter.com/2026/06/03/analysis-the-fight-over-tommy-tubervilles-residency-status-is-officially-under-way/ |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260604191430/https://www.alreporter.com/2026/06/03/analysis-the-fight-over-tommy-tubervilles-residency-status-is-officially-under-way/ |archive-date= 2026-06-04 |access-date= 2026-06-04 |work= Alabama Political Reporter |language= en-US}}</ref> The [[United States Constitution]] requires senators to live in the state where they are elected, but does not provide a minimum length of residency; Alabama requires a candidate to reside in the state for just one day to run for office.<ref name= "Kessler"/><ref name= "Labbate"/> But Alabama's Constitution requires that governors be "resident citizens of this state at least seven years next before the date of their election."<ref>{{Cite web |title= Alabama Legislature |url= https://alison.legislature.state.al.us/constitution?section=117 |access-date= 2026-10-21 |website= alison.legislature.state.al.us}}</ref> 

